+# Fork
+
+Split off a side investigation without losing your place in a long task.
+
+The `fork` tool starts a copy of the current conversation in a background `pi`
+process, gives it one task, keeps working in the parent, and adds the fork's
+answer to the parent conversation when the fork is done.
+
+```
+> refactor the parser, and check whether the other call sites have the same bug
+  ... [fork] task: check the other call sites for the same bug ...
+  ... parent keeps working ...
+  ... [fork-result] Fork fork-3f9a1c02 completed: ... ...
+```
+
+## Installation
+
+Add the extension to the `extensions` list in your agent `settings.json`
+(`~/.pi/agent/settings.json` for the user scope, `.pi/settings.json` in a
+project). Paths are resolved relative to the settings file:
+
+```json
+{
+  "extensions": ["extensions/fork/index.ts"]
+}
+```
+
+Every `pi` process that uses those settings loads the extension, including the
+forks it starts, which is what lets a fork report its result back. No other
+configuration is required.
+
+## Tool
+
+| Parameter | Type | Description |
+|-----------|------|-------------|
+| `task` | `string` | What the fork should investigate. It is the fork's only instruction. |
+
+The tool returns as soon as the fork process has started. A task that is empty
+or only whitespace is rejected and starts nothing.
+
+## Result
+
+A fork's result is a custom message in the parent session:
+
+```json
+{
+  "customType": "fork-result",
+  "display": true,
+  "content": "Fork fork-3f9a1c02 completed:\n\n<final assistant message>",
+  "details": {
+    "forkId": "fork-3f9a1c02",
+    "status": "completed",
+    "sessionFile": "/home/you/.pi/agent/sessions/--work-repo--/2026-01-01T00-00-00-000Z_fork-3f9a1c02.jsonl"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Failed forks use `"status": "failed"` and add `"error"` with the reason: the
+provider's error message when the last model response failed, otherwise why the
+process ended. The result is added exactly once. If the parent is running, its
+model sees the result in a later request of the same run; if the parent is idle,
+the result starts a new run. Aborting the parent does not lose a result: a
+result that had not reached the model yet is delivered once the parent is idle.
+
+## What a fork inherits
+
+- The conversation as the parent's model saw it when the fork was requested
+  (after a compaction, that is the compacted view), followed by the assistant
+  message that requested the fork and a tool result for every tool call of that
+  message. Sibling tool calls in that message may still be running, so they get
+  a placeholder result.
+- The parent's environment, agent directory, settings, extensions, model
+  providers, model and thinking level.
+- One instruction: the task, delivered as the fork's first user message.
+
+The fork writes its own session file in the parent's session directory, with
+`parentSession` in its header pointing at the parent session file, so you can
+resume the fork later. Nothing the parent does after the fork reaches the fork,
+and a fork cannot fork.
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+- Forks belong to the session that started them. Quitting pi or switching to
+  another session stops them within a few seconds and delivers nothing, even if
+  you later resume that session.
+- If the parent process dies without shutting down, its forks notice within a
+  second and exit.
+- `/reload` keeps forks running. The reloaded extension adopts them from the
+  session and still delivers each result exactly once.
+
+## Environment
+
+| Variable | Description |
+|----------|-------------|
+| `PI_FORK_CHILD` | Set to `1` in a fork process. Disables the `fork` tool there. |
+| `PI_FORK_ID` | Id of the fork. |
+| `PI_FORK_PARENT_PID` | Parent process a fork watches to know when to stop. |
+| `PI_FORK_CLI` | Overrides the pi CLI entry script used for forks. For tests. |
